  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.3. Increased capacity of Member States to set adequate wages and promote decent working time</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The government of Mongolia issued a resolution in June 2022 and the Minister of Education and Science issued an order in October 2022 to introduce performance-related pay in the remuneration system of the education sector at the country level for the very first time. A working group was established at the Ministry of Education and Science together with the Ministry of Labour and Social Protection and with representatives of teachers. That working group discussed the design of a performance-related pay based on the collection of data and evidence on international experiences of gender-sensitive performance-related pay in public sectors, as well as on the analysis of the key issues and challenges in the existing remuneration system. The new pay component assesses and rewards the performance of the whole range of workers in the education sector, such as low-paid auxiliary workers, administration staff, teachers, and school management. The ILO provided technical advice and recommendations to the working group - which included the relevant Ministries as well as teachers&#x2019; representatives - that led to the introduction of performance-related pay in the remuneration system of the education sector. That support included the development of a methodology handbook on defining and establishing a performance-related pay component. The ILO also commissioned a study on international evidence concerning performance-related pay which included the analysis of gender impacts and the needs of the lowest-paid workers in the education sector and which helped the development of a model and implementation mechanism appropriate for the Mongolian context. This process benefitted from support from the Government of Sweden.</narrative>
   </description>
